error[string_literal_write]: `a[0]` writes into a string literal: `a` still holds one, and literals are read-only
  --> tests/lint/string_literal_write/bad.jai:5:5
   |
 5 |     a[0] = #char "j";
   |     ^^^^
   |
   = help: copy it first, for example `a = copy_string(a);`
   = note: `string_literal_write` is `deny` by default

error[string_literal_write]: `b[1]` writes into a string literal: `b` still holds one, and literals are read-only
  --> tests/lint/string_literal_write/bad.jai:8:5
   |
 8 |     b[1] += 1;
   |     ^^^^
   |
   = help: copy it first, for example `b = copy_string(b);`
   = note: `string_literal_write` is `deny` by default

error[string_literal_write]: `b.data[2]` writes into a string literal: `b` still holds one, and literals are read-only
  --> tests/lint/string_literal_write/bad.jai:9:5
   |
 9 |     b.data[2] = #char "x";
   |     ^^^^^^^^^
   |
   = help: copy it first, for example `b = copy_string(b);`
   = note: `string_literal_write` is `deny` by default

error[string_literal_write]: `b.data.*` writes into a string literal: `b` still holds one, and literals are read-only
  --> tests/lint/string_literal_write/bad.jai:10:5
   |
10 |     b.data.* = #char "W";
   |     ^^^^^^^^
   |
   = help: copy it first, for example `b = copy_string(b);`
   = note: `string_literal_write` is `deny` by default

